WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:17.968
To Holmes she is always the woman.

2
00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:21.269
I have seldom heard him mention her by any other name.

3
00:00:22.249 --> 00:00:25.250
It was not that he felt any emotion like love for Irene Adler.

4
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:31.932
Love was a distraction to his cold and precise mind, something that might interfere with his work.

5
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.363
Yet there was only one woman who intrigued him, and that was Irene Adler.

6
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:41.147
One night as I approached our rooms on Baker Street, I looked up at the windows.

7
00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:46.811
The rooms were brilliantly lit, and I saw the silhouette of the great detective against the blinds.

8
00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:52.436
Holmes was pacing with his head sunk upon his chest and his hands clasped behind his back.

9
00:00:53.537 --> 00:00:57.440
To me, who knew his every mood and habit, it was obvious that he was working on a

10
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.044
case. I went up to our rooms, and he motioned me to an armchair by the fire.

11
00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:08.498
"You may be interested in this," he said, tossing me a sheet of thick, pink-tinted notepaper.

12
00:01:08.518 --> 00:01:11.139
"It came in the last mail delivery.

13
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:12.780
Read it aloud."

14
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.682
The note was undated and without either the writer's signature or address.

15
00:01:18.722 --> 00:01:22.884
I began to read: "There will call upon you tonight at quarter to eight o'clock a gentleman who

16
00:01:22.924 --> 00:01:26.106
desires to consult you upon a matter of the greatest importance.

17
00:01:27.246 --> 00:01:31.688
Your recent services to a royal house of Europe have shown that you may be safely trusted.

18
00:01:32.736 --> 00:01:35.157
This account of you we have from all quarters received.

19
00:01:36.218 --> 00:01:40.679
Be in your chamber at that hour, and do not be offended if your visitor wears a mask."

20
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.821
"This is indeed a mystery," I said.

21
00:01:43.841 --> 00:01:45.822
"What do you think it means?"

22
00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:49.143
"I have no data yet," said Holmes.

23
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:52.404
"It is a mistake to theorize before one has data.

24
00:01:53.225 --> 00:01:54.685
But what do you make of the note itself?"

25
00:01:56.546 --> 00:02:00.888
"The man who wrote it is wealthy," I remarked, trying to imitate my friend's thinking process.

26
00:02:02.104 --> 00:02:04.805
"This paper is expensive and peculiarly thick and stiff."

27
00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:08.886
"Peculiar—that is the very word," said Holmes.

28
00:02:08.906 --> 00:02:11.047
"It is not English paper.

29
00:02:12.127 --> 00:02:16.188
From the watermark, I have been able to determine that the paper was made in Bohemia."

30
00:02:16.208 --> 00:02:18.089
"But who wrote the letter?"

31
00:02:18.449 --> 00:02:18.849
I asked.

32
00:02:18.869 --> 00:02:22.810
"A man whose first language is German," said Holmes.

33
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:28.872
"Note this sentence: 'This account of you we have from all quarters received.'

34
00:02:29.992 --> 00:02:33.594
Only a German would be so discourteous to his verbs by leaving one at the end of a

35
00:02:33.654 --> 00:02:37.115
sentence. And here he comes if I'm not mistaken."

36
00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:43.498
As Holmes spoke, there was the sound of horses' hooves outside, followed by the ringing of our doorbell.

37
00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:47.220
While our landlady answered the door, Holmes looked out the window.

38
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.522
"A nice little carriage and a pair of expensive horses.

39
00:02:52.582 --> 00:02:55.604
There's money in this case, Watson, if nothing else," he said.

40
00:02:56.652 --> 00:03:00.513
We heard a slow, heavy step upon the stairs followed by a strong rap on the door.

41
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:02.034
"Come in!"

42
00:03:02.394 --> 00:03:02.954
said Holmes.

43
00:03:04.074 --> 00:03:06.835
The man who entered was about six feet, six inches tall.

44
00:03:07.856 --> 00:03:10.556
His dress was so rich that it bordered on bad taste.

45
00:03:11.697 --> 00:03:15.738
Heavy bands of lamb's wool decorated the sleeves and front of his double-breasted coat.

46
00:03:16.858 --> 00:03:21.880
His deep blue cloak was lined with flame-colored silk and secured at the neck with an emerald brooch.

47
00:03:22.996 --> 00:03:27.077
He carried a broad-brimmed hat and hid the upper part of his face with a black mask.

48
00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:30.038
"Please take a seat," said Holmes.

49
00:03:30.058 --> 00:03:32.659
"This is my friend and colleague, Dr.

50
00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.299
Watson, who occasionally helps me with my cases.

51
00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.340
And to whom do I have the honor of speaking?"

52
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:44.862
"You may address me as Count Von Kramm, a Bohemian nobleman," said the man with a thick German

53
00:03:44.942 --> 00:03:50.664
accent. "That is not my real name; the important person who employs me wishes my identity to remain

54
00:03:50.784 --> 00:03:51.404
unknown to you.

55
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.462
This matter involves the king of Bohemia."

56
00:03:54.482 --> 00:03:59.965
"As soon as you began to speak, I was aware of all that, Your Majesty," said Holmes, taking

57
00:04:00.026 --> 00:04:00.386
a seat.

58
00:04:01.527 --> 00:04:04.369
The man sprang from his chair and tore the mask from his face.

59
00:04:05.469 --> 00:04:06.010
"You are right.

60
00:04:07.031 --> 00:04:08.191
I am the king of Bohemia!"

61
00:04:09.232 --> 00:04:12.334
Holmes closed his eyes, seeming slightly impatient with the visitor.

62
00:04:12.354 --> 00:04:14.636
"Please state your case."

63
00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:19.920
The king looked with some surprise at the lounging figure of the detective and began his story.

64
00:04:21.736 --> 00:04:27.118
"About five years ago during a lengthy visit to Warsaw, I made the acquaintance of Irene Adler," said

65
00:04:27.158 --> 00:04:27.518
the king.

66
00:04:27.538 --> 00:04:33.139
"The name is undoubtedly familiar to you as she is well known for her attempts to advance herself

67
00:04:33.179 --> 00:04:33.839
in society."

68
00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.081
"Watson, please look her up in my index," murmured Holmes without opening his eyes.

69
00:04:40.181 --> 00:04:45.163
For many years Holmes had adopted a system of summarizing newspaper articles about people and things.

70
00:04:46.403 --> 00:04:50.224
As a result he could furnish information about almost any person or subject.

71
00:04:51.292 --> 00:04:56.937
In this instance I found Irene Adler's biography sandwiched between that of a rabbi and a naval officer

72
00:04:56.977 --> 00:04:58.878
who had written a book about deep-sea fish.

73
00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.721
"Let me see," said Holmes.

74
00:05:01.741 --> 00:05:05.204
"Born in the United States in 1858.

75
00:05:05.664 --> 00:05:06.124
A singer.

76
00:05:07.185 --> 00:05:09.787
Contralto, Imperial Opera of Warsaw.

77
00:05:10.768 --> 00:05:12.890
Retired from singing and now living in London.

78
00:05:14.791 --> 00:05:20.781
Hmm I deduce that Your Majesty became entangled with this young woman, wrote her some romantic letters, and

79
00:05:20.861 --> 00:05:22.523
now wants to get those letters back."

80
00:05:23.704 --> 00:05:25.045
"Exactly," said the king.

81
00:05:25.065 --> 00:05:28.749
"But how—" "Was there a secret marriage?"

82
00:05:29.109 --> 00:05:30.070
Holmes asked abruptly.

83
00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.094
"No." "Any legal papers or certificates?"

84
00:05:35.115 --> 00:05:38.198
"None." "Then I fail to see what the problem is."

85
00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:40.480
Holmes closed his eyes again.

86
00:05:41.652 --> 00:05:46.696
"If this woman were to produce her letters for blackmail or other evil purposes, how would she prove

87
00:05:46.776 --> 00:05:47.656
their authenticity?"

88
00:05:47.676 --> 00:05:50.698
"My handwriting," said the king.

89
00:05:50.718 --> 00:05:53.861
"That could have been forged," said Holmes.

90
00:05:53.881 --> 00:05:56.142
"My private notepaper."

91
00:05:57.163 --> 00:05:58.944
"Stolen," Holmes quickly replied.

92
00:05:58.964 --> 00:06:01.446
"My photograph."

93
00:06:01.466 --> 00:06:06.349
"Bought." "We were both in the photograph," said the king.

94
00:06:06.369 --> 00:06:08.050
"Oh, dear!"

95
00:06:08.971 --> 00:06:10.112
Holmes' eyes flew open.

96
00:06:11.048 --> 00:06:12.428
"This is very bad indeed!

97
00:06:13.429 --> 00:06:15.189
You were most indiscreet, Your Majesty."

98
00:06:15.209 --> 00:06:20.271
"I was young—I am only thirty now—and I was only the crown prince then.

99
00:06:20.291 --> 00:06:22.351
I was madly in love."

100
00:06:23.331 --> 00:06:25.972
"The photograph must be recovered," said Holmes.

101
00:06:25.992 --> 00:06:28.213
"Have you tried to buy it from her?"

102
00:06:29.293 --> 00:06:33.314
"She will not sell at any price, so my agents have made five attempts to steal it.

103
00:06:34.354 --> 00:06:40.209
Twice I hired burglars to ransack her house; one time we diverted her luggage when she traveled; twice

104
00:06:40.329 --> 00:06:41.790
she has been stopped and searched.

105
00:06:42.731 --> 00:06:45.333
But there has been absolutely no sign of the photograph."

106
00:06:47.094 --> 00:06:47.835
Holmes laughed.

107
00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:51.238
"It's quite a pretty little problem."

108
00:06:51.258 --> 00:06:55.141
"But a very serious one to me," said the king reproachfully.

109
00:06:55.161 --> 00:06:57.243
"She intends to ruin me."

110
00:06:57.263 --> 00:06:58.664
"But how?"

111
00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:04.229
"I am about to be married to Princess Clotilde, second daughter of the king of Scandinavia.

112
00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.132
You may know of the strict principles of her family.

113
00:07:08.224 --> 00:07:10.765
The princess herself is the very soul of delicacy.

114
00:07:11.786 --> 00:07:15.528
A shadow of a doubt about my conduct would bring our engagement to an end."

115
00:07:15.548 --> 00:07:18.609
"And what does Irene Adler propose to do?"

116
00:07:18.629 --> 00:07:24.212
"She threatens to send the photograph to the Scandinavian royal family, and I know she will do it

117
00:07:24.272 --> 00:07:26.933
because she has a heart of steel," said the king.

118
00:07:26.953 --> 00:07:32.076
"She wants to make sure that I do not marry another woman—and will go to any length to

119
00:07:32.156 --> 00:07:32.636
stop me!"
